Basic append-only log
- Create/open a WAL file.
- Append binary records.
- Assign monotonically increasing sequence numbers.
- Read records sequentially.
First milestone

Binary record
+------------+---------+----------+-------------+
| Record len | Version | Sequence | Payload Len |
| 4 bytes    | 1 byte  | 8 bytes  | 4 bytes     |
+------------+---------+----------+-------------+
|           Payload(UTF-8 encoded string        |
+------------------------------------------------+
|                  CRC32C                        |
|                  4 bytes                       |
+------------------------------------------------+

Record len, length of binary record
version, tells the readers what to expect, in case we introduce new fields in future
sequence number, number the tells the logical order of binary records as per the application, this should be strictly increasing by 1
payload length, indicates the length of byte[] array payload. Max payload supported is 1024x1024 i.e 1MiB. Min is 3 characters
checksum, calculates checksum for all data and adds at the end.

public interface WriteAheadLog extends AutoCloseable {

    long append(byte[] payload) throws IOException; // This can either just append or append and flush based on settings

    void flush() throws IOException; // if we append in batches, we can flush once. Only after a batch of appens are flushed, the db will say the transaction is successul

    void recover(RecordConsumer consumer) throws IOException;

    long lastSequenceNumber();

    @Override
    void close() throws IOException;
}


Crash recovery
- Recover records after restarting.
- Detect incomplete records.
- Handle partially written records.
- Truncate an incomplete tail safely.

Situation	|Action
Clean EOF	|Recovery successful
Incomplete final record	|Truncate incomplete tail
Invalid record length	|Fail recovery
Checksum mismatch	|Fail recovery
Invalid record version	|Fail recovery
Corruption followed by additional data	|Fail recovery



